[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Next: what you build it with. The repo has no build step today — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m opens straight in a browser — and
  that's worth keeping or losing deliberately, because it sets how every later change gets made and tested.

  - [1mVanilla JS, ES modules, no build[0m — [38;5;153mopen index.html[39m still just works. No [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, no bundler, nothing to break
    in six months. You hand-write DOM updates, which is fine at this size and gets tedious somewhere past a few hundred
    lines of UI.
  - [1mVite + a framework (React/Svelte/etc.)[0m — declarative rendering, much nicer as the UI grows, standard testing story.
    Costs a toolchain, a dependency tree, and a build step between you and the page.
  - [1mVanilla JS + Vite[0m — build step for dev server and bundling, no framework. Middle option that mostly buys hot reload.

  [1mMy recommendation: vanilla JS with ES modules, no build.[0m At this scope the framework would be most of the complexity
  in the repo, and the "no toolchain to rot" property is genuinely valuable for something you'll poke at occasionally.
  It's also not a one-way door — the module boundaries we'd design (storage, task operations, change events, rendering)
  are the same ones you'd keep if you later moved rendering into a framework. The one real cost: you'd write a bit of
  DOM plumbing by hand.
